// version history

//@version=6
//@description How to Choose and Modify Kernels in Practice [list] [*]Compact Support Kernels (e.g., Epanechnikov, Triangular): Use for localized smoothing and emphasizing nearby data. [*] Oscillatory Kernels (e.g., Wave, Cosine): Ideal for detecting periodic patterns or mean-reverting behavior. [*] Smooth Tapering Kernels (e.g., Gaussian, Logistic): Use for smoothing long-term trends or identifying global price behavior. [/list]
library("lib_kernel", overlay=false)

//@enum 
//@field Epanechnikov (compact kernel) Optimal for local smoothing; balances sensitivity and robustness. Suitable for detecting price reversals with minimal lag.
//@field Epanechnikov_alt (compact kernel) Optimal for local smoothing (strong focus on recent values); balances sensitivity and robustness. Suitable for detecting price reversals with minimal lag.
//@field Triangular (compact kernel) Balances between sharp and smooth responses. Useful for breakout detection and support/resistance identification.
//@field Triangular_alt (compact kernel) (needs alt_modificator) Balances between sharp and smooth responses. Useful for breakout detection and support/resistance identification. Alternative smoothing short-term noise in volatile markets. Maintains some influence from edges without abrupt transitions at |u|=1
//@field Rectangular (compact kernel) (= Uniform) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision.
//@field Uniform (compact kernel) (= Rectangular) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision.
//@field Uniform_alt (compact kernel) (needs alt_modificator) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision. Alternative adds decay or weighting factor, transitions from flat to tapered kernel. Reduces noise while maintaining a strong local focus. Useful for identifying breakouts or consolidations.
//@field Logistic (smoothing kernel) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness.
//@field Logistic_alt (smoothing kernel) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness. Alternative: Better for situations where intermediate values need emphasis, such as isolating specific price ranges or detecting mean-reverting behavior within a narrow band.
//@field Logistic_alt2 (smoothing kernel) (needs_alt_modificator) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness. Alternative2: The additional parameter controls the steepness of the sigmoid curve. Higher paramter fo focus sharply on values near u=0, Detect rapid changes in trends or momentum.
//@field Gaussian (smoothing kernel) Smooths price action while preserving underlying trend. Ideal for long-term trend identification.
//@field Gaussian_alt (smoothing kernel) (needs alt_modificator) Smooths price action while preserving underlying trend. Ideal for long-term trend identification. Skews the kernel to focus more on rising or falling prices. Detects directional bias in price data
//@field Silverman (smoothing kernel) Balances responsiveness and smoothness; used for trend detection in mixed markets (trending and ranging phases).
//@field Quartic (smoothing kernel) (= Biweight) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions.
//@field Quartic_alt (smoothing kernel) (needs alt_modificator) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions. Alternative: skews the kernel to shift emphasis left or right based on parameter. Shifts focus to rising or falling trends based on market direction. Detects asymmetry in price action.
//@field Biweight (smoothing kernel) (= Quartic) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions.
//@field Triweight (smoothing kernel) Even smoother than Biweight; ideal for detecting slow-moving trends in low-volatility markets.
//@field Wave_alt (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. Alternative variant is more suitable for localized smoothing or scenarios where only nearby data should influence the prediction (e.g., short-term price forecasting, smoothing intraday data).
//@field Wave_alt2 (oscillatory kernel) (needs alt_modificator) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. Alternative variant 2 is focused on periodic behavior within a defined range. Identify cycles in price movements without long-range artifacts.
//@field Wave (= Sinc) (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. May be used for global smoothing or scenarios where long-range correlations are important (e.g., identifying repeating patterns across long time frames).
//@field Sinc (= Wave) (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. May be used for global smoothing or scenarios where long-range correlations are important (e.g., identifying repeating patterns across long time frames).
//@field Cosine (oscillatory kernel) Smooths with minimal lag; suitable for detecting trends in range-bound markets.
//@field Cosine_alt (oscillatory kernel) Smooths with minimal lag; suitable for detecting trends in range-bound markets. Alternative: adds amplitude modulation. Reduces influence near edges, emphasizing central values. Good for smoothing momentum signals to reduce noise. Highlights central patterns in oscillating data.
export enum Kernel
    // compact kernels (detect breakouts)
    Epanechnikov
    Epanechnikov_alt
    Triangular
    Triangular_alt
    Rectangular
    Uniform
    Uniform_alt

    // smoothing kernels (detect long term trends)
    Logistic
    Logistic_alt
    Logistic_alt2
    Gaussian
    Gaussian_alt
    Silverman
    Quartic
    Quartic_alt
    Biweight
    Triweight

    // oscillatory kernels (detect recurring patterns)
    Sinc
    Wave
    Wave_alt
    Wave_alt2
    Cosine
    Cosine_alt

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//           compact kernels (detect breakouts)
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

//@description kernel_Epanechnikov (compact kernel) Optimal for local smoothing; balances sensitivity and robustness. Suitable for detecting price reversals with minimal lag.
export kernel_Epanechnikov     (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 0.75 * (1 - math.pow(u, 2))                                         : 0.0
//@description kernel_Epanechnikov_alt (compact kernel) Optimal for local smoothing (strong focus on recent values); balances sensitivity and robustness. Suitable for detecting price reversals with minimal lag.
export kernel_Epanechnikov_alt (float u, float sensitivity = 0)        => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 0.75 * (1 - math.pow(u, 2)) * math.exp(-sensitivity * math.abs(u))  : 0.0 // focus on recent price action by heavily weighting nearby values

//@description kernel_Triangular (compact kernel) Balances between sharp and smooth responses. Useful for breakout detection and support/resistance identification.
export kernel_Triangular       (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 1 - math.abs(u)                                                     : 0.0
//@description kernel_Triangular_alt (compact kernel) Balances between sharp and smooth responses. Useful for breakout detection and support/resistance identification. Alternative smoothing short-term noise in volatile markets. Maintains some influence from edges without abrupt transitions at |u|=1
export kernel_Triangular_alt   (float u, float sensitivity = 1)        => math.max(0, 1 - math.abs(u)) * (1 - math.exp(- sensitivity * math.abs(u)))

//@description kernel_Rectangular (compact kernel) (= Uniform) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision.
export kernel_Rectangular      (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 0.5                                                                 : 0.0
//@description kernel_Uniform (compact kernel) (= Rectangular) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision.
export kernel_Uniform          (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 0.5                                                                 : 0.0
//@description kernel_Uniform_alt (compact kernel) Highly responsive but may overreact to noise. Best for fast-moving markets where speed outweighs precision. Alternative adds decay or weighting factor, transitions from flat to tapered kernel. Reduces noise while maintaining a strong local focus. Useful for identifying breakouts or consolidations.
export kernel_Uniform_alt      (float u, float sensitivity = 0)        => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? math.exp(-sensitivity + math.abs(u))                                : 0.0


///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//      smoothing kernels (detect long term trends)
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

//@description kernel_Logistic (smoothing kernel) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness.
export kernel_Logistic         (float u)                               => 1 / (1 + math.exp(-u))   // globally balanced, for smoothing price data or detecting trends across a wide range of inputs
//@description kernel_Logistic_alt (smoothing kernel) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness. Alternative: Better for situations where intermediate values need emphasis, such as isolating specific price ranges or detecting mean-reverting behavior within a narrow band.
export kernel_Logistic_alt     (float u)                               => 1 / math.exp(u + 2 + math.exp(-u)) // (almost the same as gaussian) strong focus on intermediate values in a narrow range of x, to isolate specific price ranges or detect mean-reverting behaviour within a narrow band
//@description kernel_Logistic_alt2 (smoothing kernel) Effective for filtering noisy data in volatile markets. Helps smooth chaotic trends while retaining responsiveness. Alternative2: The additional parameter controls the steepness of the sigmoid curve. Higher paramter fo focus sharply on values near u=0, Detect rapid changes in trends or momentum.
export kernel_Logistic_alt2    (float u, float sigmoid_steepness = 1)  => 1 / (1 + math.exp(-sigmoid_steepness * u)) // The parameter controls the steepness of the sigmoid curve. Higher paramter fo focus sharply on values near u=0, Detect rapid changes in trends or momentum.

//@description kernel_Gaussian (smoothing kernel) Smooths price action while preserving underlying trend. Ideal for long-term trend identification.
export kernel_Gaussian         (float u)                               => math.exp(-math.pow(u, 2) / 2)
//@description kernel_Gaussian_alt (smoothing kernel) Smooths price action while preserving underlying trend. Ideal for long-term trend identification. Skews the kernel to focus more on rising or falling prices. Detects directional bias in price data
export kernel_Gaussian_alt     (float u, float sensitivity = 1)            => math.exp(-math.pow(u, 2) / 2) * (1 + sensitivity * u)

//@description kernel_Silverman (smoothing kernel) Balances responsiveness and smoothness; used for trend detection in mixed markets (trending and ranging phases).
export kernel_Silverman        (float u)                               => 0.5 * math.exp(-math.abs(u) / math.sqrt(2)) * math.sin(math.abs(u) / math.sqrt(2) + math.pi / 4)

//@description kernel_Quartic (smoothing kernel) (= Biweight) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions.
export kernel_Quartic          (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 15 / 16 * math.pow(1 - math.pow(u, 2), 2)                               : 0.0
//@description kernel_Quartic_alt (smoothing kernel) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions. Alternative: skews the kernel to shift emphasis left or right based on parameter. Shifts focus to rising or falling trends based on market direction. Detects asymmetry in price action.
export kernel_Quartic_alt      (float u, float sensitivity = 0)        => 15 / 16 * math.pow(1 - math.pow(u, 2), 2) * (1 + sensitivity * u)
//@description kernel_Biweight (smoothing kernel) (= Quartic) Robust against outliers while preserving trend sensitivity. Great for filtering during highly volatile trading sessions.
export kernel_Biweight         (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 15 / 16 * math.pow(1 - math.pow(u, 2), 2)                               : 0.0
//@description kernel_Triweight (smoothing kernel) Even smoother than Biweight; ideal for detecting slow-moving trends in low-volatility markets.
export kernel_Triweight        (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? 35 / 32 * math.pow(1 - math.pow(u, 2), 3)                               : 0.0

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//     oscillatory kernels (detect recurring patterns)
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

//@description kernel_Sinc (= Wave) (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. May be used for global smoothing or scenarios where long-range correlations are important (e.g., identifying repeating patterns across long time frames).
export kernel_Sinc             (float u)                               => u == 0 ? 1 : math.sin(u) / (u) // Sinc function
//@description kernel_Wave (= Sinc) (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. May be used for global smoothing or scenarios where long-range correlations are important (e.g., identifying repeating patterns across long time frames).
export kernel_Wave             (float u)                               => u == 0 ? 1 : math.sin(u) / (u) // Sinc function
//@description kernel_Wave_alt (oscillatory kernel) Useful for harmonic pattern detection and oscillatory price movements. Detects price cycles effectively. Alternative variant is more suitable for localized smoothing or scenarios where only nearby data should influence the prediction (e.g., short-term price forecasting, smoothing intraday data).
export kernel_Wave_alt         (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <= 0.5 ? (1 - math.abs(u)) * math.cos(math.pi * u)                                    : 0.0 // 0.5 -> limited range // (1 - |u|) -> emphazied weight on recent values + smoother edges + maximum at 1 // => limited to oszillating between 0 and 1 , compact support faster calculation
//@description kernel_Cosine (oscillatory kernel) Smooths with minimal lag; suitable for detecting trends in range-bound markets.
export kernel_Cosine           (float u)                               => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? math.pi / 4 * math.cos(math.pi / 2 * u)                                      : 0.0
//@description kernel_Cosine_alt (oscillatory kernel) Smooths with minimal lag; suitable for detecting trends in range-bound markets. Alternative: adds amplitude modulation. Reduces influence near edges, emphasizing central values. Good for smoothing momentum signals to reduce noise. Highlights central patterns in oscillating data.
export kernel_Cosine_alt       (float u, float sensitivity = 0)        => math.abs(u) <=   1 ? math.pi / 4 * math.cos(math.pi / 2 * u) * (1 - sensitivity * math.pow(u,2))  : 0.0



// Kernel Regression
//@function standard kernel functions, see enum Kernel comments and function descriptions for usage szenarios and parameters
export kernel(float u, Kernel select, float alt_modificator = na) =>
    switch select
        // compact kernels
        Kernel.Epanechnikov     => kernel_Epanechnikov(u)
        Kernel.Epanechnikov_alt => kernel_Epanechnikov_alt(u, alt_modificator)
        Kernel.Triangular       => kernel_Triangular(u)
        Kernel.Triangular_alt   => kernel_Triangular_alt(u, alt_modificator)
        Kernel.Rectangular      => kernel_Rectangular(u)
        Kernel.Uniform          => kernel_Uniform(u)
        Kernel.Uniform_alt      => kernel_Uniform_alt(u, alt_modificator)

        // smoothing kernels
        Kernel.Logistic         => kernel_Logistic(u)
        Kernel.Logistic_alt     => kernel_Logistic_alt(u)
        Kernel.Logistic_alt2    => kernel_Logistic_alt2(u, alt_modificator)
        Kernel.Gaussian         => kernel_Gaussian(u)
        Kernel.Gaussian_alt     => kernel_Gaussian_alt(u, alt_modificator)
        Kernel.Silverman        => kernel_Silverman(u)
        Kernel.Quartic          => kernel_Quartic(u)
        Kernel.Quartic_alt      => kernel_Quartic_alt(u, alt_modificator)
        Kernel.Biweight         => kernel_Biweight(u)
        Kernel.Triweight        => kernel_Triweight(u)

        // oscillatory kernels
        Kernel.Sinc             => kernel_Sinc(u)
        Kernel.Wave             => kernel_Wave(u)
        Kernel.Wave_alt         => kernel_Wave_alt(u)
        Kernel.Cosine           => kernel_Cosine(u)
        Kernel.Cosine_alt       => kernel_Cosine_alt(u, alt_modificator)
        => na


//@function standard kernel functions, see enum Kernel comments for usage szenarios. performance optimized version using fixed bandwidth and target
//@param src input data series
//@param bandwidth sample window of nearest neighbours for the kernel to process
//@param kernel type of Kernel to use for processing, see Kernel enum or respective functions for more details
//@param exponential_distance if true this puts more emphasis on local / more recent values
//@param alt_modificator see kernel functions for parameter descriptions. Mostly used to pronounce emphasis on local values or introduce a decay/dampening to the kernel output
export kernel_regression (float src = close, simple int bandwidth = 5, simple Kernel kernel, simple bool exponential_distance = false, float alt_modificator = na) =>
    
    // limit buffer access before bandwidth candles are available, if bandwidth stays the same, we can 
    int max_distance = math.min(bar_index, bandwidth - 1) // iteration starting from 0, including current bar
    float bandwidth2 = math.pow(bandwidth, 2)

    float sum_k = 0
    float sum_ky = 0

    for i = 0 to max_distance // as i is the distance from the current bar (starting from 1, or 0 if confirmed), we can use -i as replacement for (xi - x0)   
        u = exponential_distance
         ? math.pow(i,2) / bandwidth2 // allowing for more responsiveness for local/recent changes, like EMA compared to SMA
         : -i / bandwidth
        K = kernel(u, kernel, alt_modificator) // using lookup table for distance weights
        sum_ky += K * nz(src[i], 0) // = K * yi
        sum_k += K
    
    predicted_price_x0 = nz(sum_ky / sum_k, src)

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                             DEMO
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// kernel regression examples

var int  bandwidth               = input.int  (45   , 'kernel regression bandwidth', group = 'General settings', tooltip = 'window size of nearest neighbours to consider for smoothing')
var bool exponential_distance    = input.bool (true , 'use exponential_distance'   , group = 'General settings', tooltip = 'instead of using the (xi - x0) / bandwidth for distance weights, we sqare divdend and divisor to emphasize recent changes. Like EMA compared to SMA')
var bool show_0_line             = input.bool (true , 'show line 0'                , group = 'General settings' , inline = 'lines')
var bool show_u_line             = input.bool (false, '1'                          , group = 'General settings' , inline = 'lines')
var bool show_uh_line            = input.bool (false, '0.5'                        , group = 'General settings' , inline = 'lines')
var bool show_lh_line            = input.bool (false, '-0.5'                       , group = 'General settings' , inline = 'lines')
var bool show_l_line             = input.bool (false, '-1'                         , group = 'General settings' , inline = 'lines')

var bool use_Epanechnikov        = input.bool (true , 'use Epanechnikov'           , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'ep'    )
var bool use_Epanechnikov_alt    = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'ep'    )
var bool use_Triangular          = input.bool (false, 'use Triangular'             , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'triang')
var bool use_Triangular_alt      = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'triang')
var bool use_Rectangular         = input.bool (false, 'use Rectangular'            , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'unif'  )
var bool use_Uniform             = input.bool (false, 'use Uniform'                , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'unif'  )
var bool use_Uniform_alt         = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'unif'  )
var bool use_Logistic            = input.bool (true , 'use Logistic'               , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'logi'  )
var bool use_Logistic_alt        = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'logi'  )
var bool use_Logistic_alt2       = input.bool (false, 'alt2'                       , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'logi'  )
var bool use_Gaussian            = input.bool (false, 'use Gaussian'               , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'gauss' )
var bool use_Gaussian_alt        = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'gauss' )
var bool use_Silverman           = input.bool (false, 'use Silverman'              , group = 'smoothing kernels' )
var bool use_Biweight            = input.bool (false, 'use Biweight'               , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'Biweight')
var bool use_Quartic             = input.bool (false, 'use Quartic'                , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'Biweight')
var bool use_Quartic_alt         = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'Biweight')
var bool use_Triweight           = input.bool (false, 'use Triweight'              , group = 'smoothing kernels' )
var bool use_Sinc                = input.bool (true , 'use Sinc'                   , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'wave')
var bool use_Wave                = input.bool (false, 'use Wave'                   , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'wave')
var bool use_Wave_alt            = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'wave')
var bool use_Cosine              = input.bool (false, 'use Cosine'                 , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'cos' )
var bool use_Cosine_alt          = input.bool (false, 'alt'                        , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'cos' )

var float param_Epanechnikov_alt = input.float(0    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'ep'    )
var float param_Triangular_alt   = input.float(1    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'triang') // must not be 0, TODO modify formula
var float param_Uniform_alt      = input.float(1    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'compact kernels'   , inline = 'unif'  )
var float param_Logistic_alt2    = input.float(1    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'logi'  )
var float param_Gaussian_alt     = input.float(0    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'gauss' )
var float param_Quartic_alt      = input.float(0    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'smoothing kernels' , inline = 'Biweight')
var float param_Cosine_alt       = input.float(0    , 'sensitivity' , step = 0.1     , group = 'oscillatory kernels' , inline = 'cos')

price_Epanechnikov     = use_Epanechnikov     ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Epanechnikov     , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Epanechnikov_alt = use_Epanechnikov_alt ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Epanechnikov_alt , exponential_distance , param_Epanechnikov_alt ) : na
price_Triangular       = use_Triangular       ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Triangular       , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Triangular_alt   = use_Triangular_alt   ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Triangular_alt   , exponential_distance , param_Triangular_alt ) : na
price_Rectangular      = use_Rectangular      ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Rectangular      , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Uniform          = use_Uniform          ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Uniform          , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Uniform_alt      = use_Uniform_alt      ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Uniform_alt      , exponential_distance , param_Uniform_alt ) : na
price_Logistic         = use_Logistic         ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Logistic         , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Logistic_alt     = use_Logistic_alt     ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Logistic_alt     , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Logistic_alt2    = use_Logistic_alt2    ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Logistic_alt2    , exponential_distance , param_Logistic_alt2 ) : na
price_Gaussian         = use_Gaussian         ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Gaussian         , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Gaussian_alt     = use_Gaussian_alt     ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Gaussian_alt     , exponential_distance , param_Gaussian_alt ) : na
price_Silverman        = use_Silverman        ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Silverman        , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Biweight         = use_Biweight         ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Biweight         , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Quartic          = use_Quartic          ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Quartic          , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Quartic_alt      = use_Quartic_alt      ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Quartic_alt      , exponential_distance , param_Quartic_alt ) : na
price_Triweight        = use_Triweight        ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Triweight        , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Sinc             = use_Sinc             ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Sinc             , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Wave             = use_Wave             ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Wave             , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Wave_alt         = use_Wave_alt         ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Wave_alt         , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Cosine           = use_Cosine           ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Cosine           , exponential_distance ) : na
price_Cosine_alt       = use_Cosine_alt       ? kernel_regression(close, bandwidth, Kernel.Cosine_alt       , exponential_distance , param_Cosine_alt ) : na


plot(price_Epanechnikov      , 'predicted price Epanechnikov'     , color.aqua    , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Epanechnikov_alt  , 'predicted price Epanechnikov_alt' , color.blue    , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Triangular        , 'predicted price Triangular'       , color.yellow  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Triangular_alt    , 'predicted price Triangular_alt'   , color.orange  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Rectangular       , 'predicted price Rectangular'      , color.red     , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Uniform           , 'predicted price Uniform'          , color.red     , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Uniform_alt       , 'predicted price Uniform_alt'      , color.maroon  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Logistic          , 'predicted price Logistic'         , color.olive   , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Logistic_alt      , 'predicted price Logistic_alt'     , color.lime    , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Logistic_alt2     , 'predicted price Logistic_alt2'    , color.green   , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Gaussian          , 'predicted price Gaussian'         , color.purple  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Gaussian_alt      , 'predicted price Gaussian_alt'     , color.navy    , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Silverman         , 'predicted price Silverman'        , color.silver  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Biweight          , 'predicted price Biweight'         , color.white   , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Quartic           , 'predicted price Quartic'          , color.white   , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Quartic_alt       , 'predicted price Quartic_alt'      , color.gray    , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Triweight         , 'predicted price Triweight'        , color.black   , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Sinc              , 'predicted price Sinc'             , color.fuchsia , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Wave              , 'predicted price Wave'             , color.fuchsia , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Wave_alt          , 'predicted price Wave_alt'         , color.purple  , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Cosine            , 'predicted price Cosine'           , #ad8a21       , force_overlay = true)
plot(price_Cosine_alt        , 'predicted price Cosine_alt'       , #165918       , force_overlay = true)


hline(show_0_line ?  0 : na,  '0', color = chart.fg_color)
hline(show_u_line ?  1 : na,  '1', color = chart.fg_color, linestyle = hline.style_solid)
hline(show_l_line ? -1 : na, '-1', color = chart.fg_color, linestyle = hline.style_solid)
hline(show_uh_line ?  0.5 : na,  '0.5', color = chart.fg_color, linestyle = hline.style_dotted)
hline(show_lh_line ? -0.5 : na, '-0.5', color = chart.fg_color, linestyle = hline.style_dotted)
plot((close - price_Epanechnikov      ) / close, 'predicted change ratio Epanechnikov'     , color.aqua    )
plot((close - price_Epanechnikov_alt  ) / close, 'predicted change ratio Epanechnikov_alt' , color.blue    )
plot((close - price_Triangular        ) / close, 'predicted change ratio Triangular'       , color.yellow  )
